<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Reflecting a Flower Bed</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A landscape designer is planning a symmetrical garden. They place a triangular flower bed on one side of a central pathway (the \(y\)-axis, shown dashed). The vertices of the flower bed are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>A({@x1@}, {@y1@})</b></li>
  <li><b>B({@x2@}, {@y2@})</b></li>
  <li><b>C({@x3@}, {@y3@})</b></li>
</ul>
[[jsxgraph width="440px" height="440px"]]
var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
  boundingbox: [-8, 8, 8, -8], axis: true, grid: true, showCopyright: false, showNavigation: false,
  keepaspectratio: true, pan: {enabled: false}, zoom: {enabled: false},
  defaultAxes: {
    x: {ticks: {ticksDistance: 1, insertTicks: false, minorTicks: 0, drawLabels: true, label: {fontSize: 9}}},
    y: {ticks: {ticksDistance: 1, insertTicks: false, minorTicks: 0, drawLabels: true, label: {fontSize: 9}}}
  }
});
// central pathway = the y-axis
board.create('line', [[0,-100],[0,100]], {strokeColor:'#0b8043', dash:2, strokeWidth:2,
  straightFirst:true, straightLast:true, fixed:true, highlight:false});
board.create('text', [0.3, 7.4, 'pathway'], {fontSize:12, color:'#0b8043', fixed:true, highlight:false});
// original flower bed (fixed - students cannot move it)
var p1 = board.create('point', [{#x1#}, {#y1#}], {name:'A', color:'#1f6feb', fixed:true, highlight:false, showInfobox:false, label:{offset:[6,6]}});
var p2 = board.create('point', [{#x2#}, {#y2#}], {name:'B', color:'#1f6feb', fixed:true, highlight:false, showInfobox:false, label:{offset:[6,6]}});
var p3 = board.create('point', [{#x3#}, {#y3#}], {name:'C', color:'#1f6feb', fixed:true, highlight:false, showInfobox:false, label:{offset:[6,6]}});
board.create('polygon', [p1, p2, p3], {fillColor:'#1f6feb', fillOpacity:0.2,
  borders:{strokeColor:'#1f6feb', strokeWidth:2}, fixed:true, highlight:false, vertices:{visible:true, fixed:true}});
[[/jsxgraph]]
<p><b>1.</b> The designer reflects the flower bed across the \(y\)-axis to make a matching bed on the other side. Enter the coordinates of each reflected vertex.</p>
<div>\(A' = \Big(\) [[input:ans1x]] \(,\) [[input:ans1y]] \(\Big)\) &nbsp; [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<div>[[validation:ans1x]] [[validation:ans1y]]</div>
<div>\(B' = \Big(\) [[input:ans2x]] \(,\) [[input:ans2y]] \(\Big)\) &nbsp; [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<div>[[validation:ans2x]] [[validation:ans2y]]</div>
<div>\(C' = \Big(\) [[input:ans3x]] \(,\) [[input:ans3y]] \(\Big)\) &nbsp; [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<div>[[validation:ans3x]] [[validation:ans3y]]</div>
<p><b>2.</b> Is the reflected flower bed congruent to the original flower bed? &nbsp; [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h4>Worked solution</h4>
<p><b>Step 1: apply the reflection rule.</b> Reflecting a point in the \(y\)-axis reverses the sign of the \(x\)-coordinate and leaves the \(y\)-coordinate unchanged: \[ (x,\,y) \;\longrightarrow\; (-x,\,y). \] (Reflecting in the <em>x</em>-axis would do the opposite, \((x,y)\to(x,-y)\) &mdash; a common slip.)</p>
<ul>
  <li>A({@x1@}, {@y1@}) \(\rightarrow\) <b>A'({@rx1@}, {@ry1@})</b></li>
  <li>B({@x2@}, {@y2@}) \(\rightarrow\) <b>B'({@rx2@}, {@ry2@})</b></li>
  <li>C({@x3@}, {@y3@}) \(\rightarrow\) <b>C'({@rx3@}, {@ry3@})</b></li>
</ul>
<p><b>Step 2: congruence.</b> A reflection is an <em>isometry</em> (a rigid motion): it preserves every length and angle. The two flower beds therefore have identical side lengths and angles, so they are <b>congruent</b>.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
